---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "data-folder", title: "The data folder" },
  { id: "outside", title: "Outside the data folder" },
  { id: "database", title: "The database" },
  { id: "ledger", title: "Agent events" },
  { id: "redaction", title: "Redaction" },
  { id: "secrets", title: "Settings and secrets" },
  { id: "window", title: "Window storage" },
  { id: "retention", title: "How long things stay" },
  { id: "deleting", title: "What deleting removes" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout slug="storage" sections={sections}>
  <p>
    OpenOrc keeps your work on your computer, almost all of it in one data folder. It has no server of its own, and <a href={docHref("network")}>Network connections</a> lists everything that leaves your computer.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="data-folder">
    <h2 id="data-folder">The data folder</h2>
    <p>
      The folder is <code>~/Library/Application Support/OpenOrc</code> on macOS, <code>%APPDATA%\OpenOrc</code> on Windows, and <code>~/.config/OpenOrc</code> on Linux. The <code>OPENORC_USER_DATA</code> environment variable picks another folder.
      <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>Data</strong>, shows the path in use.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Path</th>
            <th scope="col">What it holds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>openorc.sqlite</code>, <code>-wal</code>, <code>-shm</code></td>
            <td>The database: threads, tasks, agent events, memories, and settings. Back up all three files together.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>attachments/</code></td>
            <td>Files you attached. Images get a random name; other files get a random ID followed by their own name.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>tool-images/</code></td>
            <td>Images that agents' tools returned, one folder per run</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>worktrees/</code></td>
            <td>Worktrees for threads and tasks, and copies of pull requests under review, one folder per project</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>workspace/</code></td>
            <td>The default folder for Workspace threads</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>orclings/</code></td>
            <td>A folder for each Orcling to work in</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>logs/provider/</code></td>
            <td>The agents' native output, kept for debugging. Streamed pieces are left out, and a line over 64 KiB is cut to a preview.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>models/</code></td>
            <td>The model that memory search runs on this computer, downloaded the first time memory needs it</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>project-icons/</code></td>
            <td>Icons OpenOrc found for your projects, and the one you chose</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>agent-processes/</code></td>
            <td>On macOS and Linux, a record of each running agent process: its ID and start time, and OpenOrc's own. The next launch uses it to stop any left behind by a crash. No prompts or credentials.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>slack-secrets.enc</code>, <code>memory-extraction-key.enc</code>, <code>github-reviewer-app.enc</code></td>
            <td>Encrypted secrets; see <a href="#secrets">Settings and secrets</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>updates.json</code>, <code>.updaterId</code></td>
            <td>Whether OpenOrc checks for updates on its own and which version you dismissed or were told about, and a random ID the update library keeps. OpenOrc sends that ID empty.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>exports/</code></td>
            <td>Patch files you exported from a task</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>task-checkout/</code></td>
            <td>Scratch copies made while applying a task to your checkout</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>task-forwardings/</code></td>
            <td>The worktrees of team tasks you hand off as independent tasks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>team-workspaces/</code> and the other <code>team-*</code> folders</td>
            <td>Workspaces, merges, forks, restores, moves, and avatars for <a href={docHref("teams")}>agent teams</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Electron keeps the app window's own browser data in the same folder, including the <a href="#window">window storage</a> below.</p>
    <Callout>
      Treat the whole folder as private. It holds your prompts, code the agents read, command output, and the <code>.env</code> files that new worktrees get a copy of. A project setting, <strong
        >Copy these gitignored files into new worktrees</strong
      >, controls which files are copied.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="outside">
    <h2 id="outside">Outside the data folder</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your repositories.</strong> Worktree branches, and refs under <code>refs/openorc/</code> that keep each turn's checkpoint, task snapshots, and team operations. See <a href={docHref("git")}>Git and worktrees</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your project's files</strong>, only when you ask: exporting a plan, adding a memory to <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, saving a file in a thread's Instructions tab, or a Git action such as committing,
        restoring a checkpoint, moving a thread, or applying a task to your checkout. The Instructions tab can also save your personal instruction file, such as <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Claude's plans folder.</strong> A Claude Code thread in Plan mode creates <code>~/.claude/plans</code>, or <code>plans</code> inside <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code>, if it doesn't exist.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Temporary folders.</strong> Private folders in your system's temporary folder: one per Claude Code process for the files that hold OpenOrc's tool address, scratch space when Codex writes a title or summary in the
        background, and throwaway Git index files. Each is deleted when its job ends.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Downloaded updates.</strong> The update library keeps a downloaded app update in your system's cache folder.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The agents' own records.</strong> Each CLI keeps its own transcripts, such as <code>~/.claude/projects</code> and <code>~/.codex/sessions</code>. OpenOrc never redacts or deletes them, and the provider may keep data on its
        servers under its own terms.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="database">
    <h2 id="database">The database</h2>
    <p>
      The database is SQLite, opened by OpenOrc's core process and nothing else. It runs in write-ahead mode with <code>synchronous = NORMAL</code>: a committed write survives an app crash, but the last moments before an operating
      system crash or power loss can be lost. The schema is a list of migrations, and <code>PRAGMA user_version</code> records how many have run. A migration that fails rolls back and stops startup.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Area</th>
            <th scope="col">Tables</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Work</td>
            <td><code>projects</code>, <code>threads</code>, <code>tasks</code>, <code>runs</code>, <code>thread_messages</code>, <code>thread_queue</code>, <code>conversation_plans</code>, <code>conversation_plan_turns</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Events</td>
            <td><code>events</code>, <code>artifacts</code>, <code>audit_events</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Changes and review</td>
            <td><code>thread_checkpoints</code>, <code>snapshots</code>, <code>review_comments</code>, <code>pull_request_reviews</code>, <code>pull_request_review_comments</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Task discussions</td>
            <td><code>task_comments</code>, <code>task_comment_attempts</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Schedules</td>
            <td><code>schedules</code>, <code>schedule_firings</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory</td>
            <td><code>memories</code>, <code>memory_files</code>, <code>session_summaries</code>, <code>extraction_jobs</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Orclings</td>
            <td><code>orclings</code>, <code>orcling_instructions</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Settings</td>
            <td><code>settings</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Search</td>
            <td><code>messages_fts</code> and <code>memories_fts</code> (SQLite full-text search), <code>memory_vec</code> (vectors for memory search)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Teams (Beta)</td>
            <td>About 40 tables named <code>orchestration_*</code> and <code>team_*</code>: team definitions, executions, messages, workspaces, and the records that make team operations safe to retry</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>threads.parent_thread_id</code> links a thread to the thread whose agent started it with <code>thread_start</code>. <code>audit_events</code> records about 70 kinds of action and who took it (you, an agent, or OpenOrc): starting
      runs, creating tasks, committing, pushing, restoring files, approval decisions, and more.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/db/src/database.ts</code>, <code>schema.ts</code>, <code>repos.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ledger">
    <h2 id="ledger">Agent events</h2>
    <p>Everything an agent does arrives as events: text, tool calls, command output, file changes, and approvals. The <code>events</code> table keeps them in order, one row each.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Events are written in batches, every 50 ms or as soon as 500 are waiting. An event still waiting when the process crashes is lost.</li>
      <li>An event over 8 KiB goes to <code>artifacts</code>, and its row keeps a short preview.</li>
      <li>Images in tool results are written to <code>tool-images/</code>, and the event keeps a link.</li>
      <li>A streamed reply arrives as many small pieces. Once the finished message is stored, the pieces in between are deleted.</li>
      <li>The agents' native output isn't stored here. It goes to <code>logs/provider/</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Message search uses <code>messages_fts</code>, which indexes the redacted text of your messages and the agents' replies, not tool output or reasoning. Every word you type must match the start of a word, up to 8 words, and the command
      palette shows the best 20 matches.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/db/src/ledger.ts</code>, <code>fragments.ts</code>, <code>search.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="redaction">
    <h2 id="redaction">Redaction</h2>
    <p>Before OpenOrc stores an agent's events, run results, the audit log, the provider log, memories, and Slack requests, it replaces text that looks like a secret with a marker such as <code>[redacted:github-token]</code>. It looks for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Known token shapes: GitHub, GitLab, Slack, Anthropic, OpenAI, Stripe, npm, and Hugging Face tokens, Google API keys, AWS access key IDs, OpenOrc device keys, JSON Web Tokens, and private keys, even one cut off before its end line.</li>
      <li>Credentials in context: the token after <code>Bearer</code> or <code>Basic</code>, the password in a URL such as <code>postgres://user:password@host</code>, and a value of 8 or more characters given to a secret name, as in <code>DB_PASSWORD=…</code>.</li>
      <li>Secret fields: in structured data, such as a tool's input, the whole value of a field with a secret name, or one named <code>cookie</code> or <code>set-cookie</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A secret name ends in api key, secret, secret key, client secret, access token, auth token, refresh token, private key, password, passwd, or authorization, in any case and with any prefix. Code that only names a secret, such as <code
        >apiKey: process.env.API_KEY</code
      >, is left alone.
    </p>
    <p>Some text is stored as written:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Text an agent reads later, such as plans, task comments, review comments, team messages, and messages you queue, which stay stored after they are sent. Redacting it would change what the agent gets.</li>
      <li>Text you write and edit: drafts, task descriptions, schedule prompts, pull request review drafts, and Orcling instructions.</li>
      <li>Everything outside OpenOrc's records, such as your project, its Git history, and the agents' own transcripts.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout>
      Redaction catches common formats, not every secret. It misses a password written in a sentence, and it sometimes hides ordinary text. It runs when text is stored, so a later improvement doesn't change what is already saved. The agent
      always receives your messages and its tool output unchanged.
    </Callout>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/db/src/redact.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="secrets">
    <h2 id="secrets">Settings and secrets</h2>
    <p>
      App settings are one JSON row in the <code>settings</code> table. The same table holds memory and text generation settings, the reviewer app's details, the Workspace entrypoint, records of Slack requests (redacted), the last usage
      reports, upkeep markers, and small records that keep an action from running twice. Each project's settings are stored on its row. <a href={docHref("settings") + "#saved"}>Settings</a> lists where each choice lives.
    </p>
    <p>Secrets never go in the database. OpenOrc encrypts them with your system's protected storage (the macOS Keychain, Windows DPAPI, or the Secret Service or KWallet on Linux) and writes each to its own file:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">File</th>
            <th scope="col">Holds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>slack-secrets.enc</code></td>
            <td>Slack tokens, device keys, and Slack connection settings</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>memory-extraction-key.enc</code></td>
            <td>The optional Anthropic API key for memory</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>github-reviewer-app.enc</code></td>
            <td>The private key of your GitHub reviewer app</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>Each file is written to a temporary file that only you can read, synced to disk, then moved into place.</li>
      <li>The window can save a secret but never read one back. The one exception is a new Slack device key, shown once so you can pass it on; the relay keeps only a hash of it.</li>
      <li>On Linux, OpenOrc refuses to save a secret when the system would store it as plain text.</li>
      <li>On macOS and Linux, development and installed builds use different keychain entries, so one can't read the other's secrets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>OpenOrc never reads or stores the agents' sign-ins. Each CLI keeps its own credentials.</p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/core/src/services/settings.ts</code>, <code>apps/desktop/src/main/protected-secrets.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="window">
    <h2 id="window">Window storage</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Local storage holds drafts and pending requests (<code>openorc.draft.*</code>), such as composer text, task descriptions and comments, and edits in the Instructions tab. It also holds the layout and Preview addresses
        (<code>openorc.layout</code>), appearance (<code>openorc.theme</code>, <code>openorc.palette</code>, <code>openorc.colors</code>, and two window transparency keys), setup progress (<code>openorc.onboarding</code>), and a few
        small choices: the project for a new thread, how you last reviewed a pull request, and which notifications were already shown. A thread's draft is also saved in the database.
      </li>
      <li>IndexedDB holds images pasted into task descriptions (<code>openorc-image-drafts</code>).</li>
      <li>
        The Preview browser has its own persistent session, shared by every Preview. It keeps the cookies and site data of pages you open there, including sign-ins, across restarts. The cookies are encrypted on disk, but on Linux without
        the Secret Service or KWallet they are only obscured; see <a href={docHref("security") + "#package"}>Security model</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="retention">
    <h2 id="retention">How long things stay</h2>
    <p>
      Almost nothing expires. Threads, events, runs, and memories stay until you delete them, and the database has no size limit. Attachments and audit log entries are never deleted. OpenOrc never archives or completes a thread on its
      own.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Data</th>
            <th scope="col">How long</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Native agent output in <code>logs/provider/</code></td>
            <td>14 days. A new file starts every 10 MiB or every day, and the oldest files go first once the folder passes 512 MiB.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Native output rows that older versions stored in the database</td>
            <td>14 days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Search entries and tool images of deleted runs</td>
            <td>Removed when you delete the thread or task. Anything left over goes in the next upkeep pass.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Attachments and audit log entries</td>
            <td>Never deleted, even with their thread</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pending approvals</td>
            <td>Held in memory only. Quitting denies them.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The upkeep pass runs 60 seconds after launch and every 6 hours after that. It also returns free space in the database to your disk. A database made by an older version, which can't return free space bit by bit, is rebuilt once at
      startup if at least 64 MiB and a quarter of the file are free space, the disk has room for the rebuild, and no rebuild failed in the last 3 days. The window then says
      <strong>Tidying up saved history. This happens once and can take a minute.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/core/src/services/ledger-upkeep.ts</code>, <code>provider-log.ts</code>, <code>packages/db/src/maintenance.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="deleting">
    <h2 id="deleting">What deleting removes</h2>
    <h3>A thread</h3>
    <p>Choose <strong>Delete thread</strong> in a thread's menu, then confirm. OpenOrc closes the thread's agent and removes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Its runs, events, run summaries, message log, plans, checkpoints, queued messages, and review comments, plus the refs that kept its checkpoints in Git.</li>
      <li>Its worktree, unless another thread still uses it, as forks and threads it started do. Uncommitted changes are committed to the worktree's branch first, and the branch stays. A pull request copy's changes are discarded instead.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These stay: tasks the thread created, threads it started, its attachments and memories, audit log entries, its lines in the provider log until they expire, and the agents' own session files.</p>
    <p>An Orcling's own conversation goes only with the Orcling. A team conversation is deleted through the team's own steps; see <a href={docHref("teams")}>Agent teams</a>.</p>
    <h3>A task</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Delete task</strong> is at the bottom of a task's page, or in the Task panel for a team task. It removes the task, its discussion, its own runs, and its own worktree unless a thread uses it. Uncommitted changes in that
      worktree are committed to its branch first, and the branch stays, unless you tick <strong>Also delete branch</strong> where the Task panel offers it. The thread that worked on the task keeps its messages and changes.
    </p>
    <h3>A project</h3>
    <p>
      Right-click a project's name in the sidebar and choose <strong>Remove project…</strong>. This takes it off the Projects list and deletes nothing: files, worktrees, and history stay, and running work and schedules continue. Add the
      folder again to bring it back.
    </p>
    <h3>An Orcling</h3>
    <p><strong>Delete</strong> followed by the Orcling's name removes its conversation, instructions, and memories. Its folder goes only if it is empty. Threads it worked in keep their history.</p>
    <Callout tone="warning">
      SQLite marks deleted rows as free space without overwriting them, so deleted text can stay in the database file until that space is reused or returned to the disk. Copies outside OpenOrc, such as the agents' own transcripts, are never
      touched.
    </Callout>
    <p class="docs-source">
      Source: <code>packages/core/src/services/threads.ts</code> (<code>delete</code>), <code>workspace.ts</code>, <code>orclings.ts</code>, <code>projects.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/handlers/thread-workspace.ts</code>,
      <code>task-delete.ts</code>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "network" }, { slug: "security" }, { slug: "settings" }, { slug: "git" }]} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
